{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>A sabbath unto HaShem</b> — for the name of HaShem, just as is said regarding the Sabbath of Creation; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 25:2). But our Rabbis did not intend this in their midrash, for all the sabbaths and also the festivals are for the name of HaShem, yet it is not said of any one of them לה׳; rather He said, \"It shall be a sabbath-rest for you\" (Leviticus 23:24), and of the Day of Atonement He said, \"A sabbath of solemn rest it is for you\" (ibid. 32). And the language of the Baraita in Torat Kohanim (chapter 1, law 2): \"A sabbath unto HaShem\" — just as is said regarding the Sabbath of Creation, so is said regarding the seventh year, \"a sabbath unto HaShem.\" But the meaning of \"a sabbath unto HaShem your Elohim\" stated regarding the Sabbath of Creation (Exodus 20:10) is that on it He rested and was refreshed, and therefore you shall not do any work; and therefore they said that the same is said regarding the shemittah, for it is the seventh among the years.\n\nNow here they have aroused us with a great secret from the secrets of the Torah. Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra already hinted to us this, when he wrote that the reason for \"a sabbath unto HaShem\" is like the day of the Sabbath, and the secret of the days of the world is alluded to in this place. And bend your ear to hear what I am permitted to make you hear of it in the language that I shall make you hear, and if you merit you will understand.\n\nI have already written in the book of Genesis (Genesis 2:3) that the six days of Creation are the days of the world, and the seventh day is a sabbath unto HaShem your Elohim (Exodus 20:11), for on it there will be sabbath for the Great Name, as we learned (Tamid 7:4): on the seventh what would they say? \"A psalm, a song for the Sabbath day\" — for the future to come, which is entirely sabbath and rest for the life of the worlds. Now the days allude to what He created in the act of Creation, and the years will allude to what will be in the creation of all the days of the world.\n\nTherefore Scripture dealt more strictly with the shemittah than with all other negative commandments, and decreed exile upon its account, just as He dealt strictly with forbidden sexual relations (Leviticus 18:28), as it says, \"Then the land shall make up for its sabbath years\" (Leviticus 26:34), and He repeated the matter many times: \"all the days of its desolation it shall keep sabbath\" (ibid. ibid. 35), and it says, \"and the land shall be forsaken by them and shall make up for its sabbath years\" (ibid. ibid. 43). And so we learned (Avot 5:9): exile comes upon account of oppression of judgment and perversion of judgment and desecration of the shemittah of the land — because whoever denies it does not acknowledge the act of Creation and the World to Come.\n\nAnd likewise the Prophet dealt strictly and decreed exile regarding the release of slaves in the seventh year, as it says (Jeremiah 34:13–14), \"I made a covenant with your fathers, etc. at the end of seven years, etc.\" — for the seventh year and the jubilee also apply to the slave.\n\nAnd the jubilee will yet be known from Genesis through \"and they were finished\" (Genesis 2:1), for in the jubilee all shall return, each man to his inheritance and each man to his family, for it is a foundation of foundations; let the believer be silent. And this is what is said: \"the land shall keep a sabbath\" (here, Leviticus 25:2), \"and you shall proclaim liberty in the land\" (Leviticus 25:10), for it is the land of the living alluded to in the first verse (Genesis 1:1), concerning which it says, \"and I will remember the land\" (Leviticus 26:42), and I have already mentioned this several times. And perhaps to this our Rabbis alluded (Rosh Hashanah 21a) in saying: Fifty gates of understanding were created in the world, and all were given to Moses except one. For every shemittah is one gate of a house, and now they informed him of the entirety of existence from beginning to end, except for the jubilee, holiness."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The phrase מוּסָד מוּסָּד (\"a foundation of foundations\") appears to be textually unusual; it is rendered hyper-literally. The phrase הַמַּאֲמִין יַחֲרִישׁ (\"let the believer be silent\") may allude to Habakkuk 2:20 or Amos 5:13, but no explicit citation is given. The phrase וְהָאָרֶץ אֶזְכֹּר cited as Leviticus 26:42 actually reads וְזָכַרְתִּי אֶת־הָאָרֶץ in the verse; Ramban quotes it in abbreviated or variant form."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:59:41.445640434Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}